course: dictionary
lesson: 5871
title: The mandible
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Scientists use special words, and mandible is one of them. You may already know the everyday word for it: jaw. «The lower jaw, or mandible, is the strongest bone of the face and the only skull bone that moves; its hinge is just in front of the ear.» The words "or mandible" tell you the two words mean the same thing.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Strongest bone and the only skull bone that moves are clues about its job: it is the part that opens and closes your mouth. Hinge is a clue too. A hinge lets something swing, like a door, so the jaw swings at the hinge in front of your ear.
explain: The word also appears in insects and birds. In an ant or a beetle, the mandibles are its biting mouthparts. In a bird, the two parts of the beak are called mandibles. So context tells you which meaning is being used.
fact: Mandible means lower jawbone.
fact: Mandibles are the biting mouthparts of insects such as ants and beetles.
fact: In birds, the mandibles are the two parts of the beak.
fact: «Mandible» is the scientific word for jaw.
fact: The mandible is the only skull bone that moves, and its hinge is just in front of the ear.
exercise: In science, «mandible» is the scientific word for which part of the body? | Ear | Knee | *Jaw | Elbow
exercise: «Mandibles are the biting mouthparts of insects such as ants and beetles.» What are mandibles on an ant? | Its wings | *Its biting mouthparts | Its legs | Its eyes
exercise: «The lower jaw, or mandible, is the strongest bone of the face.» Find and copy the word that means the same as lower jaw. | face | bone | *mandible | hinge
test: In birds, what are the mandibles? | Two bones in the wing | The claws | *The two parts of the beak | The feathers on the head
test: «Its hinge is just in front of the ear.» What does a hinge let the mandible do? | *Swing open and shut | Stay perfectly still | Glow in the dark | Stretch and grow
exam: A scientist writes that a beetle uses its mandibles to chew leaves. What are the mandibles here? | *Its biting mouthparts | Its eyes | Its wings | Its antennae
